  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1207 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nA stubborn, expansive, and anomalously strong 600-dam ridge\nremains in place across portions of the Southern Plains, Lower\nMississippi Valley, Ohio River Valley, and Southeast. Relentless\nheat will persist across the region through at least Sunday, as\ntemperatures remain 12 to 16 degrees above normal. Record-setting\nhigh temperatures are forecast across nearly the entire Mid-South\nthrough Sunday, with daily highs climbing into the upper 90s and\nlower 100s.\n\nDeterministic guidance is consistent in showing an upper-level\nlow pressure over the Mid-Atlantic tracking southwestward into\nthe Southeast late this weekend, helping to break down the ridge.\nThis transition will lower upper-level heights by nearly\n100 meters, allowing a shortwave to traverse the region on\nMonday. A weak frontal boundary will accompany this wave through\nTuesday, but no appreciable rainfall is expected.\n\nLREF guidance signals a deepening trough over the Great Basin by\nthe middle of next week, with an amplifying ridge once again\nbuilding over the Lower Mississippi Valley. ECMWF ESAT\npercentiles show 500-mb heights climbing to or above the 90th\npercentile late next week. Unfortunately, this translates to\ncontinued above-normal temperatures and dry conditions across the\nregion. Although the area is losing nearly two minutes of\ndaylight each day, 850-mb temperatures are forecast to remain near \nbetween 16 and 20C, yielding high temperatures in at least the \nupper 80s to lower 90s each day. For reference, the climatological\nhighs are normally in the lower 80s for the last week of \nSeptember. \n\nAC3",
